---
interface FAQItem {
  id?: string;
  question: string;
  paragraphs: string[];
}

const faqItems: FAQItem[] = [
  {
    id: 'status',
    question: 'What is the current stability status? Is the v1.0 wire format frozen?',
    paragraphs: [
      'OmniBuf is currently <strong>experimental</strong>. Both the <a href="/go" class="doc-link">Go</a> and <a href="/es" class="doc-link">TypeScript / JS</a> implementations pass a shared cross-language conformance and fuzzing suite, but the v1.0 wire specification and runtime APIs remain open to feedback before being permanently frozen.',
      'It is ready for evaluation, benchmarking, and internal prototypes, and early adopter feedback on the wire layout or language ergonomics is actively welcome on <a href="https://github.com/sudorandom/omnibuf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link">GitHub</a>.',
    ],
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is it named OmniBuf?',
    paragraphs: [
      'The name reflects a more complete Protobuf that goes further than the original wire encoding. Standard Protobuf requires a schema just to make sense of raw bytes, and it will never compromise on carrying field metadata on the wire.',
      'OmniBuf payloads can always be parsed into explicit types without a schema, and can optionally embed field names (Field #0) when you want full named JSON output straight from a binary packet.',
    ],
  },
  {
    question: 'Why a new format?',
    paragraphs: [
      "OmniBuf started with a simple question: what would a better Protobuf wire encoding look like today? Protobuf and JSON both work well enough for most jobs, yet both carry rough edges: Protobuf's 2001 wire format is slower than it <em>could be</em> for a binary format and is opaque without the schema, while JSON is bulky and breaks on 64-bit integers and raw binary data.",
      "At the same time, a practical philosophy for an encoding format is to decouple the wire representation from how you author your types. Protobuf's schema-first IDL is great when you want a multi-language contract, so OmniBuf keeps existing <code>.proto</code> files, <code>.pb.go</code> structs, and <code>@bufbuild/protobuf</code> descriptors working as a drop-in.",
      'However, using the Protobuf IDL does not have to be the only way to use the format. When a service just wants to serialize native structs or objects without maintaining <code>.proto</code> files or a codegen pipeline, you can define field numbers directly in code (via <a href="/go#struct-tags" class="doc-link">Go struct tags</a> or <a href="/es#define-message" class="doc-link">TypeScript schemas</a>) and produce the exact same binary wire format.',
    ],
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I have to use or rewrite .proto files?',
    paragraphs: [
      'Neither. If you already use <code>.proto</code> files, OmniBuf works directly with standard proto3 and Editions (2023, 2024) schemas without changes. If you prefer not to use an external IDL at all, you can serialize plain Go structs or TypeScript objects directly using field-number annotations and optionally embed Field #0 field names for JSON-like inspection.',
    ],
  },
  {
    question: 'What languages are supported?',
    paragraphs: [
      '<a href="/go" class="doc-link">Go</a> and <a href="/es" class="doc-link">TypeScript / JavaScript</a> are currently supported:',
      '<strong><a href="/go" class="doc-link">Go</a></strong> supports plain Go structs with <code>omnibuf:"..."</code> struct tags, existing <code>.pb.go</code> structs, and <code>protoc-gen-omnibuf</code> code generation.',
      '<strong><a href="/es" class="doc-link">TypeScript / JavaScript</a></strong> (<code>@omnibuf/core</code>, <code>@omnibuf/web</code>, <code>@omnibuf/node</code>) supports declarative <code>defineMessage</code> schemas, existing <code>@bufbuild/protobuf</code> descriptors, and <code>protoc-gen-omnibuf-es</code> code generation.',
      'The wire specification is language-agnostic, with additional language implementations planned as the specification stabilizes.',
    ],
  },
];

const stripHtml = (s: string) => s.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '');

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.paragraphs.map(stripHtml).join('\n\n'),
    },
  })),
};
---

<section
  id="faq"
  class="py-16 sm:py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto border-t-2 border-ink/15"
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight uppercase leading-none">
      Frequently asked{' '}
      <span class="font-serif italic font-normal normal-case text-[#FF4F18]">questions.</span>
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t-2 border-ink divide-y-2 divide-ink/20">
    {faqItems.map((item) => (
      <div
        id={item.id}
        class="scroll-mt-16 py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-start"
      >
        <h3 class="lg:col-span-5 text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-ink leading-snug">
          {item.question}
        </h3>
        <div class="lg:col-span-7 space-y-3.5 text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch] min-w-0">
          {item.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
            <p set:html={paragraph} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJsonLd)} />
</section>
